// Formative practice quiz for handling-401-and-403-redirect-and-sign-out. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/auth-and-review/handling-401-and-403-redirect-and-sign-out

::handling-401-and-403-redirect-and-sign-out o1 - general meaning::What is the general HTTP meaning of a 401 response?{
~The server understood and refuses, whatever your credentials#Not quite. That describes 403.
=The request lacks valid authentication credentials#Correct. That is its general meaning.
~The page does not exist#Not quite. That is 404.
~The server crashed#Not quite. That is a 5xx status.
}

::handling-401-and-403-redirect-and-sign-out o1 - contract::Under this project API contract, what should a 403 do?{
~Clear the session and go to login#Not quite. That is the reaction to a 401.
~Retry until it succeeds#Not quite. A refusal will not change with retries.
=Keep the session and show not-authorized#Correct. The user is known, and signing in again would not change the answer.
~Reload the page#Not quite. The same request gets the same answer.
}

::handling-401-and-403-redirect-and-sign-out o2 - remember the page::After a 401 you redirect to login. How can the app return people to the page they were on?{
=Pass the current location in the navigate state, and read it after login#Correct. State travels with the navigation, and useLocation reads it on the login page.
~Rely on the Back button#Not quite. That depends on the history, and it may not lead where you want.
~Store it in the JWT#Not quite. A token is for identity claims, not page state.
~You cannot, since state is cleared#Not quite. Only auth state is cleared, and the location can be passed along.
}

::handling-401-and-403-redirect-and-sign-out o2 - client vs server::The app uses an HttpOnly session cookie. Your code clears the user from state. Is the server session over?{
~Yes, the two are linked#Not quite. They are separate. Clearing state changes only the client.
~Yes, once the page reloads#Not quite. A reload keeps the cookie.
=No, ending it needs a logout request to the server#Correct. Scripts cannot delete an HttpOnly cookie, and the server has not been told.
~Yes, because React removes cookies#Not quite. React has nothing to do with cookies.
}

::handling-401-and-403-redirect-and-sign-out o3 - 403 no sign-out::A signed-in user gets a 403 on one action. Why not sign them out?{
~Because 403 means the server is down#Not quite. It means the server understood and refused.
~Because sign-out needs a 401 to be called#Not quite. There is no such technical rule. It is simply the wrong response.
~You should sign them out#Not quite. That punishes the user and does not change their permissions.
=Their session is valid, so signing out loses their place and fixes nothing#Correct. They would get the same refusal after signing in again.
}
